    </tests>
    <help><![CDATA[
GrapeTree
=========

GrapeTree is a tool for visualizing core genomic relationships among bacterial pathogens. It supports multiple tree-building methods and can handle large datasets of up to 100,000 genomes.

**Input**

Either a tabular profile file containing allele calls for each sample, or a Multi-FASTA file of aligned sequences.

**Methods**

- ``MSTreeV2`` (default): minimum spanning tree with an asymmetric distance matrix and local branch recrafting
- ``MSTree``: minimum spanning tree
- ``NJ``: FastME V2 NJ tree
- ``RapidNJ``: RapidNJ for very large datasets
- ``distance``: allelic distance matrix in PHYLIP format (no tree is computed)

**Options**

- *Distance matrix type*: ``symmetric`` (default for MSTree and NJ) or ``asymmetric`` (default for MSTreeV2); not applicable for RapidNJ and ``distance``
- *Missing data handling*: ignore missing data (default), remove columns with missing data, treat missing data as an allele, or use the absolute number of allelic differences (symmetric distance matrix only)
- *Tiebreak heuristic*: ``eBurst`` or ``harmonic`` (MSTree and MSTreeV2 only)
- *Recraft branches*: trigger local branch recrafting (on by default for MSTreeV2)
- *wgMLST mode*: optional experimental support for wgMLST schemes

**Outputs**

- Newick tree file with the phylogenetic relationships (all methods except ``distance``)
- Allelic distance matrix in PHYLIP format (``distance`` method only)

For full documentation, please go to the GrapeTree wiki (https://github.com/achtman-lab/GrapeTree)
    ]]></help>
    <citations>
        <citation type="doi">10.1101/gr.232397.117</citation>
    </citations>
</tool>
